{"id": "sevt_01DdkvemD5SggqRd2iS97Jgm", "input": {"evidence": "Source: app/routes/export.py:11-13. The unauthenticated GET /export route reads the attacker-controlled query parameter \"sort\" (default \"id\") with request.args.get and passes it through clean_identifier(), imported from the external package acme_validation (app/routes/export.py:3).\nSink: app/routes/export.py:15 puts the result straight into SQL with an f-string: f\"SELECT * FROM reports ORDER BY {column}\". It isn't parameterized, and since you can't bind identifiers, the ORDER BY value is controlled only by clean_identifier().\nSanitizer cannot be verified: acme_validation is not in the repository. A search for clean_identifier/acme_validation finds only app/routes/export.py:3 and app/routes/export.py:13. It is a pinned third-party dependency (requirements.txt:2, acme-validation==2.3.1), and its source is not in the uploaded files, so I couldn't tell whether it uses a strict allowlist/regex (e.g. ^[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*$), only quotes the value, or does nothing useful. If it passes expressions such as \"id, (CASE WHEN (SELECT ...) THEN 1 ELSE 0 END)\" or subqueries, an attacker can run blind/boolean SQL injection against the SQLite DB at DB_PATH (config.py:3). Even a strict identifier regex would still let the caller sort by any column in reports, but that is low impact.\nUnrelated observation: config.py:4 has a hardcoded cloud credential committed to source (value left out on purpose). It should be rotated and removed in a separate ticket.", "finding_id": "F-107", "recommended_fix": "Don't rely on an opaque sanitizer for SQL identifiers. Map the \"sort\" parameter to a fixed allowlist of known column names in app/routes/export.py, e.g. ALLOWED = {\"id\": \"id\", \"created_at\": \"created_at\", \"name\": \"name\"}; column = ALLOWED.get(request.args.get(\"sort\", \"id\"), \"id\"), and use only values from that set in the query. To close this ticket now, have a reviewer check the source of acme-validation 2.3.1 clean_identifier(). If it enforces a strict identifier-only allowlist/regex, downgrade to low/not_exploitable. Otherwise mark exploitable/high. Separately, rotate the hardcoded credential at config.py:4 and load it from the environment or a secrets manager.", "severity": "high", "title": "Possible SQL injection in /export ORDER BY clause: safety depends on unverified third-party clean_identifier()", "verdict": "needs_review"}, "name": "create_ticket_draft", "processed_at": "2026-10-26T20:56:42.384035Z", "type": "agent.custom_tool_use"}
