{"id": "sevt_01RGRxGaVXc3rb7wmtm8nxvH", "content": [{"text": "I've filed one draft ticket for F-107 as **needs_review**, not exploitable. I couldn't confirm the injection either way, because the one function that decides it (`clean_identifier()`) isn't in the repo.\n\n| ID | Rule | Location | Verdict | Severity | Reason |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| F-107 | SQL Injection | app/routes/export.py:15 | needs_review | high (pending review) | The `sort` query parameter on `/export` (no login required) goes into `ORDER BY` through an f-string. The only protection is `clean_identifier()` from the third-party package `acme-validation==2.3.1`. That package's code isn't in the repo, so I couldn't check what it filters. |\n\n- **What would make it exploitable:** if `clean_identifier()` lets through expressions or subqueries, an attacker can run blind SQL injection against the SQLite database. If it only allows plain column names, the risk drops to low: callers can just sort by any column in `reports`.\n- **To close the ticket:** someone needs to read the `clean_identifier()` source in `acme-validation` 2.3.1 and set the final verdict from that.\n- **Recommended fix either way:** replace the call with a fixed list of allowed column names in `app/routes/export.py`, so the query doesn't depend on the package's behaviour.\n- **Separate issue:** `config.py:4` has a cloud credential written directly into the code. I left the value out of the ticket. It should be rotated and moved to environment variables or a secrets manager, and tracked in its own ticket.", "type": "text"}], "processed_at": "2026-10-26T20:56:48.732222Z", "type": "agent.message"}
